<div class="content-section" data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-01" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-01" id="ch17-l02-teaching-01">
<h2>What stayed when the visible trait disappeared?</h2>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-01-p1" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-01-p1">Mendel crossed pea lines with round seeds and wrinkled seeds. The seeds from that cross were round, yet wrinkled seeds appeared again in the following generation. If inheritance permanently blended the two forms, how could the wrinkled form return? We need to follow what was inherited, not just what was visible.</p>
<p>Keep the distinction from lesson 01: an {{v:allele|allele}} is a version of a gene, the {{v:genotype|genotype}} is the allele combination, and the {{v:phenotype|phenotype}} is the observed characteristic. For seed shape in this model, use R for the round-associated allele and r for the wrinkled-associated allele. The genotype of the individual seed being scored determines its shape: with complete dominance, RR and Rr correspond to round, and rr to wrinkled. A heterozygote has two different alleles, so a plant grown from an Rr seed still carries r. Dominance describes the heterozygote's phenotype; it does not mean that R is more common, better or stronger.</p>
<h3>Know which plants supplied the offspring</h3>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-01-p2" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-01-p2">A {{v:true-breeding|true-breeding}} line repeatedly produces the same form of the studied trait when bred within the line under the stated conditions. Mendel established such lines before comparing contrasting forms. In our one-gene model, the true-breeding round line is RR and the true-breeding wrinkled line is rr. A line's uniform appearance alone would not establish its genotype; the breeding history matters.</p>
<p>A controlled cross identifies the plants contributing the egg and the sperm. In flowering plants, pollen carries the cells that deliver sperm to an egg; pollination is the transfer of pollen, while fertilisation is the joining of sperm and egg. Keeping track of the pollen source therefore helps establish parentage. Otherwise, an unexpected offspring might reflect an unrecorded parent rather than a failure of the inheritance model.</p>
<p>The chosen starting plants are the <strong>parental, or P, generation</strong>. Their offspring are the {{v:f1-generation|F1 generation}}, meaning first filial or first offspring generation. Mendel grew the F1 seeds into plants and allowed those plants to self-pollinate: one plant could supply both egg and sperm. Their offspring form the {{v:f2-generation|F2 generation}} in this breeding design. Crossing two F1 plants can also produce an F2. These names tell you how generations are related; they do not assign a genotype by themselves.</p>
<p>We can now state the evidence clearly: contrasting true-breeding parents, a uniform visible form in the F1, and a return of the recessive form in the F2. The next question is how an allele moves through the reproductive cells between those generations.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section" data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02" id="ch17-l02-teaching-02">
<h2>A chromosome can be copied without changing its allele</h2>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02-p1" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02-p1">An ordinary diploid cell has two chromosome sets. A <strong>homologous pair</strong> consists of corresponding chromosomes, one inherited from each parent. They carry corresponding genes at matching positions, called <strong>loci</strong>; one position is a <strong>locus</strong>. The chromosomes can carry different alleles at that position. In an Rr cell, one homologue carries R and the other carries r.</p>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02-p2" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02-p2">Before meiosis, DNA is copied. Each chromosome then consists of two attached copies called <strong>sister chromatids</strong>. Copying the chromosome carrying R produces another R-bearing chromatid, not an r-bearing one. The other homologue is copied separately. The cell has more DNA, but copying has not blended the alleles or invented a new kind of allele.</p>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02-p3" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-02-p3"><strong>Meiosis</strong> uses two divisions to reduce two chromosome sets to one. The homologues separate in the first division; the sister chromatids separate in the second, without another round of DNA copying between the divisions. A cell with one chromosome set is <strong>haploid</strong>. The inheritance result we need is that a normal <strong>gamete</strong>, an egg or sperm, carries one allele at this locus. It still carries many other genes; “one allele” does not mean one gene in the entire cell.</p>
</div>

<div class="worked-example" data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-worked" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-worked" id="ch17-l02-worked">
<p class="section-label">Worked example</p>
<h3>Follow the copies, then the separation</h3>
<p>An Rr cell copies its DNA before meiosis. Does that copying mean its gametes should carry Rr? Follow one chromosome pair to find out. Assume ordinary chromosome separation and no mutation at the locus being followed. In this simplified trace, the homologues do not exchange chromosome segments.</p>
<div class="comparison-table"><table>
<caption>One locus through chromosome copying and the two meiotic divisions</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Where the allele copies are</th><th scope="col">Why the next step matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Before copying</th><td>One homologue carries R; its partner carries r.</td><td>The pair explains why this diploid cell has two alleles at the locus.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">After copying</th><td>The R homologue has two attached R-bearing sister chromatids. The r homologue has two attached r-bearing sister chromatids.</td><td>Each copy retains its allele. Two chromatids of one copied homologue are not the two different homologues.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">After the first division</th><td>The two R-bearing sisters are in one cell; the two r-bearing sisters are in the other.</td><td>Separating homologues separates the R and r versions into different cells in this trace.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">After the second division</th><td>The sisters separate. The four haploid products carry R, R, r and r at this locus.</td><td>Each product has one copy here. The eventual gamete contribution is R or r, not the original Rr pair.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Read down the middle column while following just the r copies. They are copied and moved into different cells; the round phenotype of the seed from which the plant grew did not erase them. Then compare the last two rows. The sister chromatids remain attached after the first division. Separating those sisters finishes the reduction to one copy at the locus. The table follows chromosome reduction, rather than every stage of a plant's reproductive life cycle.</p>
<p>This is the chromosome basis of the {{v:law-of-segregation|law of segregation}}: the two alleles at a locus are separated during the production of gametes. In the simple model, a randomly chosen gamete from an Rr parent is equally likely to carry R or r. The notation Rr belongs to the parent, not to a single gamete. A homozygous parent follows the same separation process, but both homologues carry the same allele, so the gametes cannot be distinguished by that allele.</p>

<div class="content-section" id="ch17-cal-v010-l02-repair">
<h3>Repair the drawing at the point where it goes wrong</h3>
<p>Optional practice. Work on paper or think through your response; this activity is unsaved, ungraded and does not count toward chapter progress.</p>
<p>A student's drawing starts correctly with an R-bearing homologue and an r-bearing homologue. After copying, the student labels both sister chromatids of the first homologue R, and both sisters of the other r. But after the first division, the drawing places one R-bearing chromatid and one r-bearing chromatid in each new cell. The student says, “Each gamete will need both versions.” Identify the first incorrect step, describe the repair, and explain why simply changing the final gamete labels would leave the drawing misleading.</p>
<details><summary>Optional hint: distinguish a homologue from its sister copy</summary><p>At the first division, which attached structures move together? Follow the copied R homologue as a unit before separating its sister chromatids in the second division.</p></details>
<details><summary>Compare the repair and its reasoning</summary><p>The DNA-copying stage is correct. The first error is the first division: the drawing separates sisters and mixes the two homologues into both cells. In this trace, put the two attached R-bearing sisters together in one cell and the two attached r-bearing sisters together in the other. Then separate the sisters in the second division. The resulting haploid products each carry R or r at the locus.</p><p>Changing only the final labels would conceal an incorrect mechanism in the middle of the drawing. A useful repair makes both the movement and the outcome consistent: homologues separate first, sisters second, and one allele at the locus reaches each gamete. Check that your explanation identifies the first faulty stage rather than calling the original chromosome copying wrong.</p></details>
</div>
</div>

<div class="content-section" data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03" id="ch17-l02-teaching-03">
<h2>Fertilisation restores a pair, not a blend</h2>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03-p1" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03-p1">Return to the starting RR and rr plants. The RR parent contributes R at this locus and the rr parent contributes r. At fertilisation, one allele arrives in the egg and one in the sperm, restoring a pair in the new diploid cell, the <strong>zygote</strong>. Every F1 offspring in this model therefore has the combination Rr. Complete dominance accounts for its round phenotype; the r remains part of its genotype.</p>
<p>When an F1 plant supplies gametes, segregation makes R and r possible contributions. Self-pollination does not send the plant's entire Rr pair into one gamete. It brings together an egg and a sperm that have each received one allele. Under random fertilisation in this model, the egg's allele does not determine which sperm allele joins it.</p>
<p>The F2 possibilities can be read as four equally likely contributions: <strong>R from egg with R from sperm; R with r; r with R; or r with r.</strong> In the two mixed cases, the offspring is heterozygous. Rr and rR are the same genotype here; writing the inherited copies in the other order does not create another allele. Grouping the possibilities gives one RR combination, two Rr combinations and one rr combination.</p>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03-p2" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03-p2">Now apply the phenotype rule. RR and Rr are round, whereas rr is wrinkled. Thus the expected genotype ratio is <strong>1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr</strong>, while the expected phenotype ratio is <strong>3 round : 1 wrinkled</strong>. The recessive form returns when a new offspring receives r from both gametes. Its return does not require a new mutation or the reversal of a permanent blend.</p>
<h3>An expected proportion is not an offspring schedule</h3>
<p>A <strong>probability</strong> describes how likely an outcome is under a stated model. Four equally likely gamete combinations each have probability one quarter. The three round-producing combinations together account for three quarters, and the wrinkled-producing combination for one quarter. Those probabilities describe each new offspring; they do not assign a compulsory order to successive offspring.</p>
<p>For these predictions, we assume ordinary segregation, random fertilisation, the known parental genotypes and complete dominance with reliable phenotype expression. Reliable expression means the stated genotype produces the stated phenotype under the conditions being considered. We also assume the compared offspring are recorded without favouring one phenotype. A different dominance relationship, an unrecorded parent or unequal survival could change what is observed; a small difference from an expected proportion can also arise simply by chance.</p>
<p>In Mendel's seed-shape data, 5474 round seeds and 1850 wrinkled seeds gave a ratio of about 2.96:1. It was close to 3:1, not identical to it. Look at the two counts as a whole sample: no part of the model says the seeds had to appear in repeating groups. Across large samples the observed proportions tend to be closer to the modeled proportions, but the next offspring does not compensate for previous outcomes. We will use lesson 03 to organise such probabilities in grids and calculate expected numbers.</p>
<p data-canvas-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03-p3" data-canvas-helper-edit-key="ch17-l02-teaching-03-p3">Mendel used repeated breeding patterns to infer inherited units that remain distinguishable. The chromosome explanation came later. Keeping those roles separate matters: the experiment supplied the pattern; segregation and fertilisation supply a mechanism that explains it. We can test a mechanism against evidence without treating every short sample as an exact miniature of a long-run ratio.</p>

<div class="content-section" id="ch17-cal-v010-l02-records">
<h3>Would more offspring repair either problem?</h3>
<p>Optional independent practice. This supplied-record task is unsaved, ungraded and outside chapter progress.</p>
<p>Consider two hypothetical investigations. <strong>Record A</strong> identifies the seed-producing plant, but pollen from several donor plants was pooled without recording the donors. The investigator now wants to compare the offspring with a prediction for one specified parental cross. <strong>Record B</strong> identifies the fertilising gametes at a single locus: the egg carried M and the sperm carried m. M and m are two alleles of the same gene. The investigator's model nevertheless records the zygote as mm, saying, “The egg's allele can be left out because the sperm has already supplied this gene.” Both investigators propose counting more offspring as their repair.</p>
<p>Explain what is missing or incorrect in each case. Describe a repair suited to each problem, and explain what a larger sample could help with once the problem has been repaired.</p>
<details><summary>Optional hint: separate the evidence entering the model from the combination rule</summary><p>In A, ask whether you know the two parental contributions well enough to specify the proposed cross. In B, the contributions are known: follow both into the zygote. More observations and a correct account of one fertilisation have different jobs.</p></details>
<details><summary>Model, feedback and self-check</summary><p>In A, donor identity is missing. The offspring cannot safely be assigned the prediction for one chosen parental cross when several unrecorded donors may have contributed. More offspring from the same unidentified mixture do not recover that information. Use records that establish the relevant parents, or describe the mixed-parent sample honestly and obtain the missing information before applying a single-cross prediction.</p><p>In B, parentage is not the problem. Fertilisation joins an egg contribution with a sperm contribution, so the stated gametes make Mm at this locus. Writing mm discards the known M and adds a second m that the egg did not supply. Repair the allele accounting; counting more offspring cannot make that particular entry consistent with the given gametes.</p><p>Once the cross and the combination rule are sound, a larger sample can help compare observed proportions with a probability model by reducing the relative effect of chance variation. It does not guarantee an exact ratio. Your explanation should locate a missing-record problem in A and a transmission-model problem in B, propose a relevant repair for each, and state this limited benefit of increasing the sample.</p></details>
</div>
<p><strong>Carry forward:</strong> an allele can persist when its recessive phenotype is not visible. Copying preserves the allele, segregation separates the pair, and fertilisation creates a new pair. Next, we will organise those gamete combinations so that a prediction answers exactly the genotype or phenotype question being asked.</p>
</div>
